ity is a prefix: if index `i` is reachable then so is every index before it, so a single number, the farthest reachable index, captures the whole state.** Walk left to right. If `i` exceeds `farthest`, no earlier index could jump to `i` and nothing beyond it is reachable either. Otherwise update `farthest = max(farthest, i + nums[i])`. Surviving the loop means the last index is reachable.\n\n**Why it works:** from any reachable index `j <= farthest` you may jump a shorter distance, so the reachable set is exactly `[0, farthest]`; the invariant is maintained by each update. The DP view (`good[i] = any(good[j] for j reaching i)`) is O(n^2); the greedy collapses it because only the maximum matters.\n\n**Common mistakes:** depth-first search over all jumps (exponential), checking `i >= farthest` instead of `>` (fails on `[0]`), and returning early when `farthest >= n-1` without first checking the current index is reachable (fine as an optimisation, but order matters).\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Minimum number of jumps? (Jump Game II: BFS layers via the same `farthest`.) Jumps that can go left or right, or a fixed-size jump like `nums[i]` exactly? (Jump Game III: BFS/DFS with a visited set.) Return one valid sequence of jumps? (Backwards: from the end, greedily pick the leftmost index that reaches the current target.)","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["jump-game-ii","jump-game-iii","gas-station"],"number":148,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def canJump(self, nums: List[int]) -> bool:\n        pass\n"}